  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Academic affiliation</p>
    <h2>University affiliation is valuable context, not a blanket product endorsement</h2>
    <p>The Frontiers publisher record lists Genevieve Vine and Spencer Proctor with the University of Alberta, Edmonton, Canada, and Simone Persaud and Austin Swiniarski with Best Hydrate Inc., Sherwood Park, Canada.</p>
    <p>That authorship structure is useful because it makes affiliations visible. It should not be expanded into language such as “University of Alberta approved” or “University of Alberta validated” unless an institutionally authorized relationship or study result specifically supports that statement.</p>
    <p>Similarly, Dr. Proctor's academic expertise can inform scientific direction while individual Best Hydrate product claims remain subject to their own evidence and regulatory requirements.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Collaboration model</p>
    <h2>Different questions need different expertise</h2>
    <p>Hydration research can involve nutritional physiology, gastrointestinal absorption, exercise science, laboratory medicine, kidney physiology, clinical research, statistics, implementation science, manufacturing, water access and human factors. No single researcher or organization covers all of these domains.</p>
    <p>Our partnership model is therefore question-led: define the problem first, then bring in the expertise, methods and governance needed to answer it credibly.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Ways to collaborate</p>
  <h2>Research partnerships can take several forms</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Literature & synthesis</h3><p>Review established science, identify gaps and refine the questions that deserve direct testing.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Methods & protocol</h3><p>Select appropriate measures, sampling windows, controls, endpoints and analysis plans before data collection begins.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Study execution</h3><p>Conduct laboratory, field, observational or intervention work under the applicable ethics, regulatory and institutional requirements.</p></article>
    <article><h3>Translation & implementation</h3><p>Interpret findings for specific settings without extending them beyond the studied formulation, population or context.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Before a partnership begins</p>
    <h2>Set the research relationship in writing</h2>
    <p>A credible collaboration should define the research question, investigator roles, protocol ownership, data custody, funding, conflicts of interest, analysis responsibilities, authorship expectations and publication rights before results are known.</p>
    <p>For human-participant work, the agreement should also align with the applicable research ethics, informed-consent, privacy, safety and institutional requirements.</p>
    <p>These conventions protect both independence and usefulness. A collaborator should be able to report a null or unfavorable result without the relationship being redefined after the fact.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Independence principle</span>
    <h3>Good research relationships survive results the sponsor did not expect.</h3>
    <p>Methods and reporting standards should be agreed before the data are known, not rewritten to fit a preferred conclusion.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

